<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list><item>Generalized tonic-clonic seizure on a flight two and a half weeks ago, in a 36-year-old woman who was observed overnight in hospital elsewhere and has had treatment for seizures in the past, records requested; no antiseizure medication now; normal examination today. Evaluated as a new seizure: EEG and MRI of the brain with susceptibility-weighted imaging ordered, neurology (epilepsy) review booked, and no driving: active, confirmed [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Neurology (epilepsy) consultation within two weeks to interpret the EEG and MRI, decide whether this is epilepsy, and advise on antiseizure medication, surgery and safety. Guideline: Krumholz A, et al. Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults. Neurology 2015;84(16):1705-1713 (PMID 25901057) (2015); Fisher RS, et al. ILAE official report: a practical clinical definition of epilepsy. Epilepsia 2014;55(4):475-482 (PMID 24730690) (2014) [S]</item><item>Evaluation of a seizure in an adult: history from her and witnesses&apos; accounts, examination, blood count, electrolytes, glucose, calcium, magnesium, kidney and liver tests and creatine kinase, a pregnancy test, a sleep-deprived EEG, and MRI of the brain with an epilepsy protocol including susceptibility-weighted imaging; records of her earlier seizure care requested. Guideline: Krumholz A, et al. Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults. Neurology 2015;84(16):1705-1713 (PMID 25901057) (2015) [S]</item><item>Safety from today: no driving, and the report of a disorder characterized by lapses of consciousness that California requires of physicians, explained to her before it is sent; no swimming alone or bathing in a tub, showers instead; no heights or working alone near water; seizure first aid and a written seizure action plan, calling emergency services for a seizure lasting five minutes or more. Guideline: Krumholz A, et al. Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults. Neurology 2015;84(16):1705-1713 (PMID 25901057) (2015); Governor&apos;s message returning Senate Bill 357 without signature (physician reporting of disorders characterized by lapses of consciousness remains mandatory under Health and Safety Code section 103900) (2024) [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>
